window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-da8632d349c1bcaa"]={"key":"ny_b1-da8632d349c1bcaa","group":"ny_b1","uid":"da8632d349c1bcaa","rank":61,"title":"lanternwood wardenby waste explainer","occupation":"news_analysts_reporters_journalists","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"You are the local affairs journalist at Lanternwood Media preparing this week’s resident explainer for Wardenby County. I’m Hedda Rivenburgh, the commissioning editor, and I need a publication-ready article on the contested reorganisation of the county household-waste contract with Wardenby Manufacturing. Two council groups describe the change in incompatible terms, while residents mainly want to know what will actually happen at the kerb. The article will also be used verbatim by frontline resident-services staff, some of whom have followed the dispute closely and some of whom know nothing beyond the current collection routine. It must give the latter enough context without turning into a waste-policy tutorial for everyone else.\n\nUse `wardenby_waste_reorganisation_source_pack.docx`, which contains the adopted documents, attributed statements, comparison figures, applicable Wardenby County rules as at 14 March 2025, and Lanternwood’s house style. Compare the current and reorganised arrangements and explain the material changes for residents. Separate what is settled from what remains disputed or unknown, and attribute every contested number or characterisation to its source rather than laundering it into Lanternwood’s voice. Residents often use annual contract price divided by household count as a quick like-for-like test; that shortcut is already circulating in this debate even though the service scope and household base change between the periods. Handle that complication clearly enough that readers understand where the comparison remains useful and where it stops being reliable.\n\nWrite no more than 1,200 words, including the headline, any standfirst or subheadings, and the source note. Keep the tone neutral and plain, do not recommend a side or tell the council what action to take, and do not add facts from outside the supplied pack. The piece should stand alone for resident distribution and be easy for frontline staff to scan when answering routine questions. Save the finished article in the top level of your working directory as exactly `wardenby_waste_reorganisation_explainer.docx`.\n\n---\n\nThe files referred to above are in `references/`:\n\n- `references/wardenby_waste_reorganisation_source_pack.docx` — The complete offline source pack containing the adopted record, current and reorganised service details, period-comparison figures, attributed group statements, unresolved matters, applicable Wardenby County rules as at 14 March 2025, and Lanternwood Media house style.\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `wardenby_waste_reorganisation_explainer.docx` — A publication-ready, neutral resident explainer comparing the current and reorganised household-waste arrangements. (Must be saved at the top level of the working directory under the exact filename; maximum 1,200 words including headline, standfirst, subheadings, and source note; must rely only on the supplied source pack; must follow the supplied Lanternwood Media house style and present facts without recommending action.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[{"name":"package/public/reference_files/wardenby_waste_reorganisation_source_pack.docx","bytes":40017,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/3daeec476e20b10efae7baf651e51b259022de7cc69769a62114081519381b59.docx","sha256":"3daeec476e20b10efae7baf651e51b259022de7cc69769a62114081519381b59","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"LANTERNWOOD MEDIA | COMMISSIONING SOURCE PACK\nWardenby household-waste contract reorganisation\nFor the resident explainer commissioned by Hedda Rivenburgh. Information cut-off: 14 March 2025.\n1. Adopted decision record\nProvenance: County Cabinet decision record, 10 March 2025 (adopted). The Cabinet approved a variation to the Wardenby County household-waste contract with Wardenby Manufacturing and adopted the reorganised service specification. The decision is settled; operational schedules and some property-level arrangements remain to be published.\nApproved effective dates: standard kerbside properties move to the new pattern on 1 June 2025. Rural and difficult-access assisted rounds move on 1 September 2025. Communal flats remain on their existing communal collection pattern while eligibility and infrastructure for a separate food-caddy service are assessed; no start date was adopted for that element.\n2. Current service baseline (contract year to 31 March 2025)\nMeasure\nCurrent arrangement\nProperties in contract base\n86,400 occupied homes\nResidual waste\n180-litre wheeled bin, collected weekly\nMixed recycling\n240-litre bin, collected fortnightly\nFood waste\nNo separate county collection; food placed in residual bin\nGarden waste\nOptional paid fortnightly service, April-November; separate charge\nPresentation rule\nBins out by 6am on collection day; lids closed; no side waste\nThe current contract price is £8.64 million a year for residual and mixed-recycling collections for the 86,400-home base. Garden subscriptions are outside that price.\n3. Approved reorganised service specification\nProvenance: adopted specification, 10 March 2025. “Standard kerbside” means houses and converted houses on ordinary vehicle rounds. “Rural/difficult-access” means properties on assisted or remote rounds identified by county officers. Communal flats are a separate cohort.\nProperty cohort\nFrom\nResident-facing arrangement\n78,600 standard kerbside homes\n1 Jun 2025\nResidual 180L bin fortnightly; mixed recycling 240L bin weekly; separate food caddy weekly; garden waste fortnightly Apr-Nov included in the contract\n5,800 rural/difficult-access homes\n1 Sep 2025\nResidual 180L bin every three weeks; mixed recycling 240L bin every three weeks; separate food caddy weekly where the round is vehicle-accessible; assisted presentation remains available\n2,000 communal flats\nNo adopted date\nExisting communal residual and recycling pattern continues. Food-caddy eligibility, storage and collection method are pending assessment\nThe reorganised contract price is £10.80 million a year for 90,000 properties. It includes separate food collection and the scheduled garden service for eligible cohorts. The household base includes 3,600 additional properties brought into the contract count.\nCounty officers’ like-for-like analysis isolates residual and mixed-recycling service for the same 86,400-home base: £8.64 million in the current year versus an estimated £7.78 million under the new design, or about £100 versus £90 per home. This is an analytical comparison, not a separate contract price, and excludes the newly included food and garden services.\n4. Attributed statements and projections\nProvenance: statements supplied to Lanternwood Media, 12–13 March 2025. These are positions, not independent findings.\nAdministration group: “This is a £2.16 million investment in a cleaner, simpler service.” The group says weekly recycling, food collection and included garden rounds will reduce residual waste and calls the change “modernisation, not a cut”. The £2.16 million figure is the difference between the two annual contract totals; the wording is the group’s characterisation. A projected 18% reduction in residual tonnage in the first full year appears in the adopted business case; it is a projection, not an observed result.\nOpposition group: “Residents are being asked to pay 20% more for less frequent collections.” Its calculation divides £10.80 million by 90,000 properties (£120) and £8.64 million by 86,400 homes (£100). The group says the three-weekly rural round is evidence of a cut. The calculation changes the service bundle and denominator, so it is not a like-for-like measure of the same service.\nCounty officers’ analysis supports a narrower point: on the same 86,400-home base and residual/recycling scope, the estimated core cost is about 10% lower (£90 versus £100 per home). It does not establish that every household will pay less, because the contract is not a household bill and the new service includes additional streams and cohorts.\n5. Unresolved matters at the cut-off\nProvenance: implementation register, updated 14 March 2025. Not yet published or fixed: exact collection-day calendars; bin-lid colours and replacement timetable; the food-caddy liner standard for each property cohort; communal-flat food-service eligibility, storage and method; and the transition process for assisted collections. Residents should not infer these details from the adopted pattern.\n6. Wardenby County rules in force on 14 March 2025\nResidents must present containers at the property boundary by 6am on collection day, with lids closed and no side waste. Food caddies may be lined with newspaper or a county-approved compostable liner; plastic carrier bags are not permitted. Assisted collections continue for residents registered under the county’s assisted-collection process. These rules are current as at the cut-off; future implementation guidance may add practical detail.\n7. Lanternwood Media house style | resident explainers\nElement\nHouse style\nHeadline\nSentence case; name the practical change, not a disputed slogan\nStandfirst\nOne plain-language sentence of 25–35 words\nHeadings\nShort sentence case subheads organised around resident questions\nNumbers\nUse numerals for measurements, money and ages; use £ and million; percentages as whole numbers unless source uses decimals\nQuotes\nUse short exact quotations; identify speaker/group in the same sentence; do not stack rival quotes\nAttribution\nKeep contested claims attributed close to the claim; distinguish adopted fact, estimate, projection and opinion\nUncertainty\nUse “pending”, “not yet published” or “projection” where applicable\nSource note\nEnd with: “Source note: adopted county decision and service specification; current-service and period-comparison figures; attributed group statements; implementation register; and Wardenby County rules in force at 14 March 2025. Information cut-off: 14 March 2025.”\nInternal use: this pack is the complete offline record for the article. No external research is required or permitted.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/wardenby_waste_reorganisation_explainer.docx","bytes":39444,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/bda1712b894dc9cab53fbb9dd9005fa7f90f770eda6f44bf70f6e1b5f8183527.docx","sha256":"bda1712b894dc9cab53fbb9dd9005fa7f90f770eda6f44bf70f6e1b5f8183527","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"Wardenby waste collections: what changes, when and what is still undecided\nA county contract variation has been adopted. Most kerbside homes will see different collection frequencies and new food and garden services from June; some details and one property group remain unresolved.\nWardenby County Cabinet adopted a variation to its household-waste contract with Wardenby Manufacturing on 10 March 2025. That settles the broad service design, but not every practical detail residents will need. The change affects what goes out at the kerb, how often it is collected and which properties are covered.\nWhat changes for most houses?\nFor 78,600 standard kerbside homes, the new pattern starts on 1 June 2025. Residual waste in the 180-litre wheeled bin moves from weekly to fortnightly. The 240-litre mixed-recycling bin moves from fortnightly to weekly. A separate food caddy is collected weekly, and the previously optional paid garden service becomes a fortnightly April-to-November service included in the contract.\nThe county’s current arrangement, covering the contract year to 31 March 2025, has no separate food collection: food goes in residual waste. Garden collections are a separate paid subscription. The existing presentation rules remain important: containers go at the property boundary by 6am, lids closed, with no side waste.\nNot every property has the same timetable\nThe 5,800 rural or difficult-access homes on assisted or remote rounds move on 1 September 2025. Their residual and mixed-recycling collections become every three weeks, while a weekly food-caddy service is planned where the round is vehicle-accessible. Assisted presentation remains available.\nCommunal flats, about 2,000 properties, stay on their existing communal residual and recycling pattern for now. Their food-caddy eligibility, storage and collection method are still being assessed, and no start date was adopted for that service. The new pattern is therefore not a single universal experience.\nWhat is the argument about the money?\nThe current annual contract price is £8.64 million for 86,400 occupied homes and residual and mixed-recycling collections. The reorganised price is £10.80 million for 90,000 properties and also includes food collection and scheduled garden service. The extra £2.16 million is a difference between contract totals, not a household bill.\nThe opposition group describes this as residents paying 20% more for less frequent collections. Its arithmetic divides each contract total by its period household base: £100 per current home and £120 per reorganised property. That shortcut is useful for showing the groups’ competing claims, but it is not a like-for-like verdict here: both the service bundle and the household denominator change.\nCounty officers’ narrower analysis compares the same 86,400-home base and only the residual and mixed-recycling scope. It estimates £7.78 million under the new design, about £90 per home versus £100 currently, or roughly 10% lower. That is an analytical estimate, not a separate contract price, and it does not show that every household will pay less. Once food, garden service, additional properties or different rural frequencies are included, the simple per-household quotient stops being a reliable comparison.\nThe administration group calls the £2.16 million difference between annual totals an “investment” and says the change is “modernisation, not a cut”. The adopted business case projects an 18% reduction in residual tonnage in the first full year; that is a projection, not a measured result. These are attributed positions, not Lanternwood conclusions.\nWhat is settled, and what is pending?\nSettled: the Cabinet decision, the three property cohorts, the service frequencies described above and the two effective dates. Pending at the 14 March 2025 information cut-off: exact collection-day calendars; bin-lid colours and the replacement timetable; the food-caddy liner standard for each cohort; communal-flat food arrangements; and the transition process for assisted collections. Residents should not treat those details as final until the county publishes them.\nFood caddies may be lined with newspaper or a county-approved compostable liner. Plastic carrier bags are not permitted. The county’s current rules otherwise require closed lids, no side waste and presentation by 6am; future implementation guidance may add practical instructions.\nSource note: adopted county decision and service specification; current-service and period-comparison figures; attributed group statements; implementation register; and Wardenby County rules in force at 14 March 2025. Information cut-off: 14 March 2025.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The required DOCX exists at the exact top-level filename and opens as a valid, non-corrupt Word document.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted file and OOXML package structure.","grader_hint":"Confirm `wardenby_waste_reorganisation_explainer.docx` is present at the working-directory root and can be opened or parsed as DOCX."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The complete article contains no more than 1,200 words when the headline, standfirst, subheadings, captions, and source note are included.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All visible article text in the submitted DOCX.","grader_hint":"Count visible editorial words across the complete document, excluding only technical document metadata. The total must be 1,200 or fewer."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The headline and opening identify the reorganisation’s resident-facing significance without adopting either council group’s contested label as fact.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Headline, standfirst, and opening paragraphs compared with the attributed labels in the source pack.","grader_hint":"Look for a direct description of the change and why residents should care. A disputed slogan may appear only if clearly attributed or marked as disputed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article correctly identifies the formal decision status and distinguishes the adopted reorganisation from implementation details that have not yet been fixed.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision record and unresolved-matters material in the source pack versus the article.","grader_hint":"Compare the verbs used in the article with the source pack’s status labels. Approved elements must not be described as merely proposed, and pending details must not be presented as final."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The effective or planned timing stated in the article agrees with the dated implementation record, including any distinct dates that apply to different changes.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Implementation dates in the source pack and all timing claims in the article.","grader_hint":"Verify every stated start date, transition period, or staged date against the controlling dated material. Do not accept collapsing separate dates into one if that would mislead residents."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The description of the current collection arrangement accurately reflects the supplied baseline period and does not mix it with features of the reorganised arrangement.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Current-service record in the source pack versus the article’s baseline description.","grader_hint":"Check collection streams, cadence, container or presentation rules, and included properties against the source-designated current period."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The description of the reorganised arrangement accurately captures every material resident-facing service change documented in the source pack.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Approved service comparison in the source pack versus the article.","grader_hint":"Identify all source-pack changes likely to alter what a resident puts out, when it is put out, what container or stream is used, or which service applies. Material cases should not be omitted."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Where the effects differ by household or property category, the article identifies the affected categories and does not imply one universal experience.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Eligibility and property-scope material in the source pack versus resident-facing statements in the article.","grader_hint":"Check that any documented differences for property types, service cohorts, exceptions, or transition groups are made visible in proportion to their practical importance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The current and reorganised periods are compared on matching measures where a valid comparison is available, with units and time bases kept consistent.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period-comparison figures and definitions in the source pack versus the article’s comparisons.","grader_hint":"For every comparative number, verify that units, covered services, period length, and population basis match or that the mismatch is explicitly qualified."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article explains why the commonly used contract-price-per-household shortcut is not fully like-for-like here and states the boundary beyond which it becomes unreliable.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope, denominator, and cost-definition notes in the source pack and the corresponding explanation in the article.","grader_hint":"Credit requires more than saying the figures differ. The article should identify the relevant change in included service scope and household base, and should distinguish any limited valid use from the invalid inference."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every contract cost, saving, increase, reduction, percentage, or per-household figure is numerically faithful to the source and retains the source’s period and scope.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Financial figures and definitions in the source pack versus all financial claims in the article.","grader_hint":"Trace each financial number. Derived arithmetic is acceptable only if reproducible from supplied figures, calculated correctly, and labelled so it does not appear to be an official source figure."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"All contested figures and evaluative characterisations are attributed to the person, group, document, or analysis that supplied them.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Provenance labels and attributed statements in the source pack versus the wording of the article.","grader_hint":"Inspect claims involving success, failure, savings, cuts, service quality, risk, or political intent. Attribution must be close enough that a reader cannot mistake the claim for Lanternwood’s conclusion."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article uses its own evidence-led structure rather than presenting alternating quotations or mechanically giving each group equal space.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Overall article structure and use of quotations or paraphrases.","grader_hint":"Judge whether resident questions and evidentiary status organise the piece. Quotes should support explanation, not substitute for it, and space should reflect relevance rather than artificial symmetry."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The principal point of disagreement between the two council groups is represented accurately, with each position tied to the different premise, measure, or interpretation shown in the source pack.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The two groups’ statements and the underlying comparison material in the source pack versus the article.","grader_hint":"Check that the disagreement is not flattened into two unexplained slogans and that neither position is strengthened beyond its supplied evidence."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Material unknowns and pending decisions identified in the source pack are explicitly labelled as unknown, pending, or not yet published.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Unresolved-matters register in the source pack versus the article.","grader_hint":"Verify that each unresolved matter with a practical resident consequence is acknowledged and that the article does not fill gaps through prediction or inference."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article accurately states the Wardenby County presentation or collection rules that residents need in order to understand the changed arrangement as of 14 March 2025.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Applicable jurisdictional rule extracts in the source pack versus the article.","grader_hint":"Check only rules that materially clarify resident obligations or service eligibility. Wording may be simplified, but conditions, exceptions, and the as-of-date position must remain accurate."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Technical waste, procurement, and governance terms needed for comprehension are translated into plain language at first use without turning the article into a general tutorial.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Terminology in the source pack and its treatment in the article.","grader_hint":"A reader new to the debate should understand necessary terms from concise context. Do not require definitions of familiar words or reward lengthy background unrelated to what changes."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article serves both uninformed residents and readers already familiar with the dispute: essential context is present, but it does not crowd out the current change and comparison.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The complete article, especially the opening and background passages.","grader_hint":"Assess whether a first-time reader can follow the piece without outside explanation while an informed reader reaches the substantive update quickly."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Names, organisations, dates, service labels, quoted phrases, and numerical claims remain internally consistent throughout the article.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Repeated references within the submitted article, checked against the source pack.","grader_hint":"Look for drift between headline, body, callouts, and source note, including inconsistent period labels or different values for the same measure."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The tone remains neutral and precise, making clear distinctions between documented fact, attributed opinion, projection, and uncertainty without recommending a side or policy action.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The complete article.","grader_hint":"Review verbs, adjectives, headline framing, transitions, and closing language. Credit a factual resident explainer, not advocacy, a verdict on the groups, or advice to decision-makers."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every externally checkable factual claim is supported by the supplied source pack, and the article does not introduce outside facts, unsupported causes, or speculative consequences.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Claim-by-claim comparison between the article and source pack.","grader_hint":"Sample all sections, with particular attention to causal language and statements about likely behaviour or outcomes. Ordinary connective prose need not be sourced, but substantive claims must be traceable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article includes a concise source note that identifies the supplied categories of primary material and gives the information cut-off date of 14 March 2025.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Source note in the submitted article and the source inventory in the pack.","grader_hint":"Accept house-style wording rather than a formal bibliography, provided the note accurately identifies the material relied upon and contains the exact cut-off date."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document follows the supplied Lanternwood Media house style for resident explainers, including its treatment of headings, numbers, quotations, attribution, and source notes.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"House-style section of the source pack and the rendered article.","grader_hint":"Apply only conventions explicitly supplied in the pack. Judge consistent editorial application rather than pixel-perfect imitation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The rendered document is publication-ready and practical for frontline staff to scan and reuse without asking the author for clarification.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered DOCX, including hierarchy, paragraphing, spacing, tables or callouts if used, and overall readability.","grader_hint":"Inspect the rendered pages for clipping, broken elements, dense undifferentiated text, unclear navigation, or editing debris. The article should support quick retrieval of routine resident answers while still reading coherently from start to finish."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"Lanternwood Media produces civic explainers that residents and frontline council staff can use without additional briefing. The article concerns a politically contested but partly settled reorganisation of Wardenby County’s household-waste contract.","difficulty_sources":["Separating adopted facts, attributed political interpretations, projections, and unresolved implementation details","Explaining a misleading rule-of-thumb comparison whose numerator, denominator, and service scope change between periods","Serving readers with unequal background knowledge within a strict 1,200-word limit","Maintaining neutral, close attribution while producing a coherent article rather than a sequence of competing quotations","Applying fictional jurisdictional rules and local house style solely from one compiled source pack"],"authoring_plan":{"reference_artifact":{"artifact_id":"ref_source_pack","format":"DOCX","content_requirements":["Create one coherent commissioning pack whose component extracts are clearly distinguishable and whose factual record is complete. Include an adopted decision record, the current service baseline, the approved reorganised service specification, implementation timing, financial comparison material, attributed statements from two council groups, an unresolved-matters register, applicable Wardenby County rules as at 14 March 2025, and Lanternwood Media’s resident-explainer house style.","Use only the allowed person names for attributed speakers or document owners. Use Lanternwood Media as publisher and Wardenby Manufacturing as the contracted organisation. Other parties should be role labels such as administration group, opposition group, cabinet, or county officers rather than newly invented named organisations or people.","Make all underlying records mutually consistent. The two groups may offer incompatible descriptions, but those descriptions must be explicitly attributed interpretations rather than accidental contradictions in the authoritative record.","Mark provenance, status, reporting period, units, scope, and cut-off date clearly enough that every article claim can be traced offline.","Include a compact house-style specification carrying any arbitrary conventions the solver is expected to reproduce, including preferred treatment of headline and standfirst, numerals, percentages, quotations, attribution, uncertainty language, and the wording pattern or content requirements for a source note."]},"planted_professional_tensions":[{"tension":"The commonly used annual-contract-price-per-household shortcut does not remain fully comparable.","construction":"Provide current-period and reorganised-period totals for contract price and household base, but change both the bundle of services included in the contract price and the property population included in the household denominator. Also provide at least one narrower, consistently defined component that can be compared validly. The article should therefore explain both the limited use and the stopping point of the shortcut rather than rejecting all comparison.","gold_relationship":"Any reproduced quotient must equal the relevant supplied contract amount divided by the relevant supplied household base within ordinary rounding tolerance, but a valid like-for-like conclusion may be drawn only where service scope, property coverage, and time basis match."},{"tension":"The audience has uneven prior knowledge without room for a tutorial.","construction":"Include a small number of essential technical terms and governance distinctions that require brief plain-language context, alongside substantial nonessential historical or procedural background. A strong article defines only what is needed to understand the resident-facing change and decision status.","gold_relationship":"The finished article must let a first-time reader distinguish the current arrangement, approved change, disputed interpretation, and pending details while devoting most of its limited word budget to the present comparison and practical effects."}],"factual_relationships_for_gold":["The current-period description must be derived only from the source-designated baseline, and the reorganised-period description only from adopted or approved material.","If implementation is staged, each affected service or household category must be paired with its own supplied effective date rather than a single blended date.","Every material resident-facing change is one that alters collection cadence, accepted stream, presentation method, container, property eligibility, exception handling, or transition timing.","Financial differences and percentages must use matching periods and definitions. Any solver-derived number must be arithmetically reproducible from supplied inputs and clearly presented as a calculation rather than an official quoted figure.","Claims made by either council group remain claims even when accompanied by a number. They must not be rewritten as Lanternwood findings unless the underlying primary material independently establishes them.","The formal decision record controls what is settled; the unresolved-matters register identifies what remains unknown. Group statements cannot change either status.","Applicable jurisdictional rules are those marked effective as of 14 March 2025. Superseded or future-draft text, if included for context, must be visibly labelled and must not be presented as current.","The source note must describe the categories of supplied material actually used and state the 14 March 2025 information cut-off."],"imperfections_to_include":["Place the most quotable political descriptions before some of the underlying definitions so the pack rewards careful provenance reading rather than quotation assembly.","Present one group’s cost comparison with correct arithmetic but a changed denominator and broadened scope; present the other group’s rebuttal with a narrower valid point but an overbroad rhetorical conclusion.","Include at least one headline-friendly projected operational outcome that is explicitly a projection rather than an observed result.","Include pending operational details that frontline staff and residents would naturally ask about, with a clear statement that they were not fixed by the cut-off date.","Include nonessential background that is accurate but would make a 1,200-word article feel like a tutorial if copied wholesale.","Use closely related but distinct terms for the contract, service specification, and collection arrangement so the solver must preserve their meanings."],"reference_solution_behaviour":["Produce a stand-alone resident explainer that begins with the practical significance and formal status, then compares current and reorganised arrangements around resident questions.","Make settled, disputed, and unknown propositions distinguishable through wording and attribution rather than merely through equal quotation space.","State every material household-category exception and staged timing case supported by the pack.","Explain the per-household comparison boundary using the supplied scope and denominator evidence, while retaining any narrower valid comparison.","Use concise definitions for necessary terminology, omit nonessential tutorial background, and close on the present factual position rather than a recommendation or next-steps demand.","Render cleanly as a reusable Lanternwood article within the full 1,200-word budget."],"intended_verdict":"A publishable solution is accurate, source-traceable, neutral, concise, and operationally useful to residents and frontline staff. A merely plausible article should lose substantial credit if it repeats rival slogans, treats unlike financial measures as comparable, blurs adopted and pending matters, omits affected household categories, or supplies unsourced certainty."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"mixed_media_bundle","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[{"artifact_id":"ref_source_pack","filename":"wardenby_waste_reorganisation_source_pack.docx","purpose":"The complete offline source pack containing the adopted record, current and reorganised service details, period-comparison figures, attributed group statements, unresolved matters, applicable Wardenby County rules as at 14 March 2025, and Lanternwood Media house style.","required":true,"discovery":"Read the document and its tables, captions, footnotes, provenance labels, and dated extracts; no external research is required or permitted."}],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"resident_explainer","filename":"wardenby_waste_reorganisation_explainer.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A publication-ready, neutral resident explainer comparing the current and reorganised household-waste arrangements.","required":true,"constraints":["Must be saved at the top level of the working directory under the exact filename; maximum 1,200 words including headline, standfirst, subheadings, and source note; must rely only on the supplied source pack; must follow the supplied Lanternwood Media house style and present facts without recommending action."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"professional_report","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"news_analysts_reporters_journalists","sector":"information","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":4.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"A domain-expert local-government journalist should conduct the primary editorial review, followed by an independent source-trace and rendered-document check. Reviewers should grade only against the supplied pack and ordinary professional journalism practice, not outside knowledge.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["Word counts can vary slightly by office application, so grading should use visible OOXML document text and include all editorial elements named in the constraint. Visual quality remains a professional judgment, and the fictional jurisdiction must not be supplemented with real-world local rules."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:43:52+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:43:52+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:43:52+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:43:52+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:43:52+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/da8632d349c1bcaa","declared_deliverables":["wardenby_waste_reorganisation_explainer.docx"]};
